														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1420234">
									

<p>It was ahead of its time in quality and precision, no doubt; the main reason that it stood alone was because the vast majority of the populace didn't even consider something like mechanical precision. If they were to go into a massive city temple for the first time, make an offering into a box, and suddenly fountains sprang to life and horns blared and two giant statues of the gods *came to life and pulled the doors open to reveal Zeus in all his glory*, people in ancient Rome would likely shit themselves in awe and terror, rather than ask (as we would today) where all the gears and ropes and such were hidden, which is totally how they did that sort of thing. So it's more a case of (to use TVTropes style phrasing) no blueprint, no prototype, no backup.</p>
<p>That said&#8230;. To the original topic, I do still like the CONCEPT of the thing you wrote up, just this bit here irked me.</p>

